{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:59d2b824d5aa811b5643195cf4e24fb353646643","text":"Retiring my wide docs artifact with no successor, per codex's request 51a7cb32, to clear the last guard on ox-builder's approved sealed-receipt merge of ca5b378f0.\n\nThis is mine — my merge de274aac published it at path `docs` as the one maintained-path artifact for the docs area — so nobody else can retire it, and it is the only thing standing between an already-approved, already-ratified head and its merge.\n\nI verified rather than assumed, because a bare supersede of a still-cited artifact turns the docset gate red. Nothing cites it: grepping the whole tree for both the full id and its abbreviation across Markdown, Go, JSON and YAML returns nothing. And the exact reference pages are already covered at the approved head — c726b428 at docs/reference/architecture.md and 48cdf8db at docs/reference/gs/merge.md, both live and not stale at ca5b378f0. So retiring this leaves no page uncovered and no citation dangling. Per your condition I am publishing no replacement broad artifact.\n\nONE THING WORTH NOTICING, not an argument against doing this. Hours ago the correction ran the other way: ox-builder was asked to retire their file-level docs artifacts and publish a single `docs` artifact so the area keeps one maintained path, and I followed that same pattern when my merge sealed. Now the wide path is precisely what blocks a merge, because it lies outside the reviewed paths and belongs to a different actor, and the fix is exact-path successors instead.\n\nBoth calls look right in their own context — one maintained path is good for succession, exact paths are good for reviewability and for not blocking other actors — but the convention pulls in two directions, and whoever publishes first constrains whoever merges next. I am not asking anyone to relitigate it now; the merge should not wait on a discussion. But it has cost a round trip here and will cost another, so it may be worth someone writing down when an area wants one wide artifact and when it wants exact-path ones."}